= Castellation and snapping <chapter-3>

// The Castellated Meshing Process

The generation of a spatial grid within `snappyHexMesh` begins with the castellation stage, which transforms the initial background hexahedral grid into a refined, step-like Cartesian mesh. Controlled via the `castellatedMeshControls` sub-dictionary within `snappyHexMeshDict`, this phase systematically subdivides base cells across targeted regions and subsequently excises cells that fall outside the active computational domain. The initial background grid, constructed with `blockMesh`, serves as the dimensional datum from which all subsequent cell splitting occurs. Because `snappyHexMesh` employs an octree-based spatial division structure, each refinement level bisects a hexahedral cell in three spatial directions, yielding a two-to-one volumetric splitting ratio that produces eight sub-cells per parent cell at each successive refinement tier.

// Spatial and Feature-Based Refinement Controls

Local mesh density during castellation is governed through three primary refinement mechanisms: feature edge refinement, surface refinement, and volume region refinement. Feature edge refinement targets sharp topological edges and geometric intersections previously computed by the `surfaceFeatures` utility and stored in an `.eMesh` file. The user defines explicit integer refinement levels within the `features` list to ensure that geometrically critical ridges and corners receive sufficient cell density.

Following feature refinement, surface refinement is applied according to parameters specified in the `refinementSurfaces` dictionary. Surfaces can be assigned minimum and maximum refinement levels. The minimum level enforces uniform refinement across the entire surface patch, whereas the maximum level is automatically applied to cells intersecting surfaces where the local geometric curvature or intersection angle exceeds the threshold defined by the `resolveFeatureAngle` parameter. If distinct topological regions are embedded within the surface file, local surface refinement levels can be overridden for specific sub-regions within the nested `regions` sub-dictionary.

Volumetric refinement is managed through the `refinementRegions` sub-dictionary, referencing analytical shapes or auxiliary surface meshes declared in the `geometry` sub-dictionary. Users can refine cells inside a shape using the `inside` mode, outside using the `outside` mode, or progressively away from a boundary using the `distance` mode, which accepts pairs of spatial distances and target refinement levels ordered from nearest to farthest. Furthermore, when resolving narrow gaps or internal flows, the `insideSpan` mode leverages surface closeness data generated by `surfaceFeatures` to enforce a specified number of cells across the geometric span.

// Refinement Growth and Cell Budget Management

To prevent sharp discontinuities in cell size between different refinement tiers, the `nCellsBetweenLevels` keyword specifies the number of transition buffer layers added between adjacent octree levels. Maintaining a typical buffer of three cells ensures a gradual geometric transition across the domain. Because octree refinement scales cell counts exponentially, global budget constraints must be configured to avoid memory exhaustion. The `maxLocalCells` and `maxGlobalCells` parameters define the maximum allowable cell counts per processor and across the entire mesh during refinement prior to cell removal. If the refinement criteria require fewer cells than the threshold specified by `minRefinementCells`, the iterative surface refinement halts.

// Domain Identification and Cell Removal

Once feature, surface, and region splitting are complete, `snappyHexMesh` executes the cell removal phase to extract the desired fluid or solid domain. The active domain is identified by a spatial coordinate vector specified under the `locationInMesh` or `insidePoint` parameter within `castellatedMeshControls`. The algorithm retains cells where approximately fifty percent or more of the cell volume resides within the contiguous region enclosing the designated point, while all disconnected or external cells are pruned. To ensure proper domain identification, this coordinate point must reside unambiguously within the fluid volume and must never lie directly on a CAD boundary surface or coincide with a cell face. The resulting castellated mesh forms a topologically valid, step-like Cartesian discretization of the physical domain.

// Surface Snapping Mechanics and Control Parameters

Although mathematically valid, a purely castellated mesh exhibits jagged, stair-stepped boundaries that are ill-suited for accurate boundary physics and aerodynamic force calculations. The snapping phase, governed by the `snapControls` sub-dictionary, morphs the surface vertices of the Cartesian grid onto the CAD geometry to construct a smooth, body-fitted boundary. The execution sequence displaces boundary vertices onto the target triangulated surface, solves a displacement relaxation equation across the internal mesh, evaluates whether geometric quality criteria are violated, and scales back the displacement of violating vertices until all quality metrics are satisfied.

The snapping procedure is fine-tuned through several key iteration parameters in `snapControls`. The `nSmoothPatch` setting governs the initial smoothing iterations performed on boundary patches prior to identifying surface correspondence. The `tolerance` keyword defines the attraction distance ratio within which points are drawn to surface feature edges relative to the local maximum edge length. Internal mesh movement is iteratively relaxed using the `nSolveIter` parameter, while `nRelaxIter` dictates the maximum number of snapping relaxation cycles permitted when correcting quality violations.

// Snapping Optimization and Workflow Practicalities

Standard default settings in `snapControls` typically achieve acceptable body-fitted meshes for uncomplicated surfaces. However, when dealing with complex geometries characterized by high local curvature, thin trailing edges, or tight clearances, default snapping iterations may struggle to conform cleanly to boundaries without distorting cells. In such scenarios, increasing the number of solving and relaxation iterations, refining local surface levels, or modifying the background `blockMesh` resolution around critical features helps facilitate smooth surface conformance. Because castellation and snapping are modular, users can switch the `castellatedMesh` and `snap` boolean flags independently in `snappyHexMeshDict`, permitting visual inspection and iterative adjustment of the castellated template before executing the computationally demanding snapping and relaxation routines.

